# Next.js 16 App Conventions

This is Next.js 16.4 with React 19 — route `params` and `searchParams` are
Promises, and several APIs differ from older versions. Consult
`node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before using an unfamiliar API.

## Route handlers

```ts
// ✅ params is a Promise in this version
export async function PATCH(
  req: NextRequest,
  ctx: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> },
) {
  const { id } = await ctx.params;
}

// ❌ silently breaks the build
export async function PATCH(req: Request, ctx: { params: { id: string } }) {}
```

- Return `NextResponse.json(...)`; set status explicitly on errors.
- Disk/DB routes: `export const runtime = 'nodejs'` and
  `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'`.
- Parse the body defensively: `await req.json().catch(() => ({}))`, then clamp
  every numeric field. Don't echo raw errors — return `{ error: string }`.

## Components

- `'use client'` only when the file uses hooks, state or browser APIs. Keep page
  files thin and push logic into `lib/`.
- Data flows through route handlers under `app/api/**`; don't import `lib/network`
  server code into a client component.
- Server-only modules stay un-imported from client trees.

## Styling

- Tailwind v4 with project utility classes defined in `app/globals.css`:
  `.card`, `.btn`, `.hd`, `.seg`, `.g`, `.kpi`, `.tb`, `.tr`. Reuse them; don't
  invent parallel component styles or inline hex colors.
- Icons come from `components/icons.tsx`. Add a new icon there rather than
  pulling in an icon package.
- UI language is Thai; keep numbers, ids and table headers stable for copy/paste.

## TypeScript

- Strict mode. `any` is acceptable only at the JSON boundary (`req.json()`),
  and should be narrowed immediately.
- Shared shapes live in `lib/network/types.ts`; import with the `@/` alias.
